            src="assets/foca-video-dark.webp"
            data-i18n-alt="fcAltVid"
            alt="Video lesson screens with captions, audio description, transcript and notepad options, and the floating next-lesson bar."
            width="736"
            height="552"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature">
        <div class="media">
          <img
            class="lt"
            src="assets/foca-feedback.webp"
            data-i18n-alt="fcAltFb"
            alt="Mock exam results split by subject, with an encouragement card suggesting a plan adjustment."
            width="736"
            height="552"
            loading="lazy"
          /><img
            class="dk"
            src="assets/foca-feedback-dark.webp"
            data-i18n-alt="fcAltFb"
            alt="Mock exam results split by subject, with an encouragement card suggesting a plan adjustment."
            width="736"
            height="552"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="copy-col">
          <h3 class="t-h5" data-i18n="fcD3t">Instant feedback, by subject</h3>
          <p data-i18n="fcD3">
            When a simulado (mock exam) ends, the results break down by subject instead of showing only a total
            score, so candidates can see where they’re losing points. This idea comes from the original prototype.
            What’s new is an encouragement card for people who pass but struggle in one or two subjects: it
            suggests shifting study hours toward them for a few days, with a shortcut to adjust the plan.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature flip right">
        <div class="copy-col">
          <h3 class="t-h5" data-i18n="fcD4t">Small wins keep people going</h3>
          <p data-i18n="fcD4">
            Studying for a concurso (public service exam) is a long haul, so small wins matter. When candidates
            complete their daily study time, a celebration screen marks it with a summary of the day and an option
            to share. It only appears after they finish the current study activity, so it never interrupts a
            lesson or a simulado.
          </p>
